Here's a clean, production-ready implementation of a sticky header using CSS and JavaScript.

## CSS

```css
/* Sticky header base styles */
.header {
  position: fixed;          /* keeps it pinned to the top of the viewport */
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  right: 0;
  width: 100%;
  z-index: 1000;            /* stays above page content */
  background-color: #ffffff;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease, box-shadow 0.3s ease;
}

/* Optional: change appearance after scrolling (e.g., solid on scroll) */
.header.scrolled {
  background-color: #f7f7f7;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 12px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18);
}

/* Header content layout */
.header__inner {
  max-width: 1200px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 16px 24px;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: space-between;
}

.header__logo {
  font-size: 1.25rem;
  font-weight: 700;
  text-decoration: none;
  color: #333;
}

.header__nav {
  display: flex;
  gap: 24px;
  list-style: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}

.header__nav a {
  text-decoration: none;
  color: #555;
}
```

## JavaScript

```javascript
const header = document.querySelector('.header');
const scrollThreshold = 20; // px of scroll before the "scrolled" state applies

function handleScroll() {
  if (window.scrollY > scrollThreshold) {
    header.classList.add('scrolled');
  } else {
    header.classList.remove('scrolled');
  }
}

// Run on initial load and while scrolling
window.addEventListener('scroll', handleScroll);
window.addEventListener('load', handleScroll);
```

## HTML (for context)

```html
<header class="header">
  <div class="header__inner">
    <a href="/" class="header__logo">Brand</a>
    <nav>
      <ul class="header__nav">
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="/products">Products</a></li>
        <li><a href="/about">About</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>
```

## Key points

- **`position: fixed`** is what makes the header "sticky" — it stays pinned to the viewport while content scrolls beneath it.
- **`z-index`** ensures the header renders above other page elements.
- **Padding on body/content**: because the header occupies space at the top, add `padding-top` (equal to the header height) to your main content so it isn't hidden behind the fixed bar.
- **`scroll` listener** toggles a class for dynamic styling (e.g., a solid background after scrolling).

## Alternative: pure CSS scroll-snap sticky

If you only need basic pinning without JavaScript, `position: sticky` handles it natively:

```css
.header {
  position: sticky;
  top: 0;
  z-index: 1000;
}
```

